1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Fire breathing tractors that haul 60,000 pounds through the dirt.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Rope that pulls, tows, and hauls hundreds of tons.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Tug boats that maneuver mega cargo ships through the narrowest of waterways.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Diamond that cuts through metal, concrete, and marble.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
And plastic that stops a speeding bullet.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:24,000
What do they all have in common?

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
They are the world's strongest.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:45,160
Strength is a powerful word, but what does it mean?

10
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:48,160
If you want the strength to resist being pulled apart,

11
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:50,160
try this rope.

12
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:54,660
For impregnability, try super strong platter.

13
00:00:54,661 --> 00:00:56,960
Or if you need the strength of the hardest material

14
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:58,900
in the world, try Diamond.

15
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,100
But if you want the strength of raw horsepower,

16
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,120
hop on the world's strongest tractor.

17
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:17,220
This is the toughest class in the world of racing.

18
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:19,660
You come out and win a Grand National Unlimited hook,

19
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:20,920
you've done something.

20
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:22,320
That's going in the history book.

21
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:29,500
Tractor pulling is about noise, adrenaline, and competition.

22
00:01:29,501 --> 00:01:30,900
It's a big rush.

23
00:01:30,901 --> 00:01:31,900
It's fun.

24
00:01:31,901 --> 00:01:36,100
We may only hit 30, 40 miles an hour, but it feels like you're going 250.

25
00:01:36,101 --> 00:01:39,100
It's got a mind of its own.

26
00:01:39,101 --> 00:01:41,900
Once it throttles down, you're along for the ride.

27
00:01:41,901 --> 00:01:42,900
But they're a lot of fun.

28
00:01:42,901 --> 00:01:43,900
They're a good ride.

29
00:01:43,901 --> 00:01:47,900
The goal is simple.

30
00:01:47,901 --> 00:01:54,220
Pull an ever-increasing load of up to 60,000 pounds and go until you can't move the weight

31
00:01:54,221 --> 00:02:01,540
any further. But there's nothing simple about these machines.

32
00:02:01,541 --> 00:02:05,260
At the National Tractor Pulling

33
00:02:05,261 --> 00:02:10,620
Championship in Bowling Green, Ohio, pullers from around the globe buy to be declared the

34
00:02:10,621 --> 00:02:18,600
strongest. This is a new tractor. It's got six motors on it. They're 572 cubic inch.

35
00:02:18,601 --> 00:02:19,740
Each motor's

36
00:02:19,741 --> 00:02:22,480
putting out about 1,700, 1,800 horsepower.

37
00:02:22,481 --> 00:02:24,340
I hope it to be the strongest one here.

38
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:31,480
I've built 14 tractors in my career,

39
00:02:31,481 --> 00:02:34,180
and this is by far the strongest tractor I've ever had.

40
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:40,060
Limited only by their imagination and pocketbook,

41
00:02:40,061 --> 00:02:43,300
these pullers build their masterpieces from the ground up.

42
00:02:43,301 --> 00:02:45,240
These here tractors, they're all,

43
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:47,440
you make them yourself and your own shop,

44
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:49,780
and there is not two unlimited tractors the same.

45
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,840
Everybody's got a different combination, different tires,

46
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:54,960
but everybody makes their own tractor.

47
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:01,560
Tractor pulls are divided into 15 classes,

48
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:06,100
but the strongest and wildest is the unlimited modified class.

49
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:11,220
This tractor right here is an unlimited,

50
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,380
and the only thing that we've got to worry about

51
00:03:13,381 --> 00:03:16,840
is length of the vehicle, weight of the vehicle,

52
00:03:16,841 --> 00:03:21,140
and tire size. There are three main types of

53
00:03:21,141 --> 00:03:25,540
engines used in the Unlimited class. There's the classic V8,

54
00:03:25,541 --> 00:03:29,180
just a little more hopped up, and you can have a lot more than

55
00:03:29,181 --> 00:03:33,300
one. There's the B12 Allison engine pulled from World War II

56
00:03:33,301 --> 00:03:36,420
fighter planes. It's a little harder to find,

57
00:03:36,421 --> 00:03:40,400
but worth the wait. And of course, the jet turbines

58
00:03:40,401 --> 00:03:43,260
that might have powered a Chinook helicopter not too long ago.

59
00:03:43,261 --> 00:03:53,960
What we have in our unlimited modified tractor is four what we call automotive type V8s.

60
00:03:53,961 --> 00:03:59,000
You won't find these engines in any automobile made today or ever made.

61
00:03:59,001 --> 00:04:02,080
These are pretty much a thoroughbred racing engine.

62
00:04:02,081 --> 00:04:08,260
Each engine will dyno out at approximately 2,800 to 3,000 horsepower.

63
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:17,260
They run on straight methanol for fuel.

64
00:04:17,261 --> 00:04:20,260
They're supercharged, fuel injected.

65
00:04:20,261 --> 00:04:22,260
What we're doing with a supercharger,

66
00:04:22,261 --> 00:04:26,260
we're taking atmospheric pressure and compressing it.

67
00:04:26,261 --> 00:04:31,260
In other words, mother nature gives us 14 pounds per square inch.

68
00:04:31,261 --> 00:04:36,260
This takes that and makes it 45 pounds per square inch

69
00:04:36,261 --> 00:04:40,260
and forces the fuel, air mixture into the cylinder,

70
00:04:40,261 --> 00:04:45,260
creates more cylinder pressure, which makes more horsepower.

71
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:46,900
Over three times the air pressure

72
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,500
means over three times as much oxygen available

73
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:52,200
for combustion.

74
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:54,400
As they line up for the full pull,

75
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:58,120
these monsters of the track pack more than 12,000 horsepower.

76
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:04,120
What kind of a movable object can meet

77
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:06,660
this irresistible force?

78
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:08,960
Enter the tractor pull sled.

79
00:05:08,961 --> 00:05:13,860
When the tractor pull or when the pulling vehicle starts pulling, we have a weight box

80
00:05:13,861 --> 00:05:16,120
here that is loaded with weight.

81
00:05:16,121 --> 00:05:22,620
And as the vehicle starts to move, this drives the weight box forward.

82
00:05:22,621 --> 00:05:27,460
As this comes forward, with further down the track we go, it hits a pair of trigger switches

83
00:05:27,461 --> 00:05:31,520
here that drops the back of the pan to start that.

84
00:05:31,521 --> 00:05:36,400
As the box goes forward, it gets to the top of the box, we have a push down that goes

85
00:05:36,401 --> 00:05:39,100
at the back of the pan to transfer 100% of the weight

86
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:44,940
of our sled to stop that momentum that they have going.

87
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:48,580
The entire 60,000 pounds of weight is on the pan,

88
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:50,440
and that pan is digging into the ground.

89
00:05:54,260 --> 00:05:56,380
Although the sled has its own driver,

90
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:59,820
the weight box slides automatically on its own track,

91
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:04,320
so everyone gets equal abuse during the pull.

92
00:06:04,321 --> 00:06:08,120
This sport may use engines that generate thousands of horsepower,

93
00:06:08,121 --> 00:06:11,320
but its origins go straight back to the horse.

94
00:06:11,321 --> 00:06:15,320
Tractor pulling's origins came from the 1920s, late 20s,

95
00:06:15,321 --> 00:06:18,320
when farmers were actually starting to get horses,

96
00:06:18,321 --> 00:06:21,320
and they would have these horse pulls, who had the strongest horse,

97
00:06:21,321 --> 00:06:24,320
then along came tractors, who had the strongest tractors.

98
00:06:24,321 --> 00:06:29,320
And then in 1969, NTPA was formed out of a group of several states

99
00:06:29,321 --> 00:06:33,320
wanting to uniform the rules so that people could go from county to county,

100
00:06:33,321 --> 00:06:39,320
state to state, nationwide to go pulling in various divisions.

101
00:06:39,321 --> 00:06:45,320
15 unlimited modified tractors run in the final event.

102
00:06:45,321 --> 00:06:47,320
There's a method to this madness.

103
00:06:47,321 --> 00:06:49,320
You have to have the front end off the ground

104
00:06:49,321 --> 00:06:51,320
because you want all the weight on the tractor

105
00:06:51,321 --> 00:06:53,320
transferring to the rear tires.

106
00:06:53,321 --> 00:06:56,320
You don't want the front end come standing way up straight,

107
00:06:56,321 --> 00:06:58,320
but you don't want it tagged to the ground either.

108
00:06:58,321 --> 00:07:01,320
Plus, transfer as much weight off the sled

109
00:07:01,321 --> 00:07:03,360
the sled onto the rear tires as possible.

110
00:07:03,361 --> 00:07:06,860
In the unlimited class, we weigh 7,900 pounds,

111
00:07:06,861 --> 00:07:10,560
and we're pulling a sled that probably weighs 60,000 pounds.

112
00:07:10,561 --> 00:07:13,860
So it's quite a tug of war.

113
00:07:13,861 --> 00:07:15,660
There's no clock to contend with.

114
00:07:15,661 --> 00:07:19,780
It's all about distance.

115
00:07:19,781 --> 00:07:24,980
Will the Victor be the V8-powered Stampede?

116
00:07:24,981 --> 00:07:29,220
Or Indian Outlaw with its turbines?

117
00:07:29,221 --> 00:07:32,720
or maybe money pit with the World War II Allison engines.

118
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:41,200
As they line up to connect to the sled,

119
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:46,800
it all comes down to strength, ingenuity, and a little luck.

120
00:07:46,801 --> 00:07:56,980
luck.

121
00:07:56,981 --> 00:08:03,340
Indian Outlaw has a bad run and nearly goes off the track.

122
00:08:03,341 --> 00:08:06,480
Money Pit comes close.

123
00:08:06,481 --> 00:08:10,600
Stampede makes its bid.

124
00:08:10,601 --> 00:08:13,740
But American Thunder with five Chevy V8 engines

125
00:08:13,741 --> 00:08:17,280
the course record at 311 feet.

126
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:20,040
Today, American Thunder, owned by Bill Voorhees,

127
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:22,480
is the world's strongest tractor.

128
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:23,880
I'm a competitive guy.

129
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:24,980
You know, you like that win.

130
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:26,680
You know, if I was hauling around a loser,

131
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:27,680
I'd probably quit.

132
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:30,500
But as long as we keep winning, we'll keep going.

133
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:32,400
But he better watch out.

134
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:36,600
His competitors will be back, more powerful than ever,

135
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:40,400
trying to claim the title of the world's strongest.

136
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:42,040
It's just an adrenaline rush.

137
00:08:42,041 --> 00:08:46,780
It's something that is really an American icon.

138
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:49,520
Engines, gears, and clutches may be thrilling

139
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:54,020
and an assault on the senses, but in the world of the strongest,

140
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:58,020
there's a special material you can barely see that someday

141
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:01,400
might protect you from a speeding bullet.

142
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,460
One of the most common examples of strength

143
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:06,940
is tensile strength, the amount of stress a material can

144
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:08,640
withstand before tearing apart.

145
00:09:08,641 --> 00:09:11,780
apart.

146
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:14,640
And this is the strength we see every day with rope.

147
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:21,160
Strong rope is made from synthetic or natural fiber.

148
00:09:21,260 --> 00:09:22,980
But there could be a whole new material

149
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,120
to make the strongest rope in history.

150
00:09:26,220 --> 00:09:28,620
In fact, one of the strongest materials in the world

151
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:32,660
is thinner than a human hair and probably resides in your backyard

152
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:36,000
right now, spider silk.

153
00:09:36,001 --> 00:09:41,300
Spider silk is an incredibly special class of materials.

154
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:43,140
For one thing, if you just think about it

155
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:45,000
from the perspective of a spider,

156
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:50,040
it's a material that spiders rely on to reproduce, to eat,

157
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:51,940
to protect themselves from predators.

158
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:56,360
It has so many remarkable mechanical properties.

159
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:58,460
Cheryl Hayashi hopes to replicate

160
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:01,000
those mechanical properties.

161
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,500
An expert in spider silk genetics,

162
00:10:03,501 --> 00:10:06,140
She leads a research team at the University of California,

163
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:07,940
Riverside.

164
00:10:08,040 --> 00:10:10,800
There are so many potential uses of spider silk.

165
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:13,580
You could have spider silk being incorporated into ropes.

166
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:16,180
And people are also interested in using spider silk

167
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:19,520
for anti-ballistic purposes, because spider silk can absorb

168
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:22,220
a tremendous amount of energy.

169
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:24,520
What makes spider silk so unique

170
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:28,220
is the combination of strength and its ability to stretch.

171
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:32,260
And that comes in really handy for certain types of rope.

172
00:10:32,261 --> 00:10:35,560
This string is composed of hundreds of spider silk fibers

173
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:38,660
and is over three years old.

174
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:41,700
Not only has it maintained stretch,

175
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:45,600
but it also constricts when it gets wet.

176
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:47,880
Imagine a rope that tightens its hold

177
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:50,380
with a simple spray of water.

178
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:54,320
According to Hayashi, stretch, or extensibility,

179
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:57,560
is integral to a major component of strength,

180
00:10:57,660 --> 00:10:59,320
toughness.

181
00:10:59,321 --> 00:11:02,320
DR. NANI NARRAJANI, Spider silk is extremely tough.

182
00:11:02,321 --> 00:11:04,820
Now, toughness refers to the amount of energy

183
00:11:04,821 --> 00:11:07,820
that can be absorbed by a certain volume of material.

184
00:11:07,821 --> 00:11:11,360
And for fibers, toughness, you could think of it

185
00:11:11,361 --> 00:11:14,360
as coming from a combination of how strong

186
00:11:14,361 --> 00:11:18,400
a particular material is and also how far it can extend.

187
00:11:18,401 --> 00:11:22,440
If you could combine high strength with high extensibility,

188
00:11:22,441 --> 00:11:25,440
then there's a tremendous amount of energy that can be absorbed

189
00:11:25,441 --> 00:11:27,940
before that material failed.

190
00:11:27,941 --> 00:11:30,840
Spider silk has been measured to be five times stronger

191
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:34,780
than steel, and some spider silk can stretch over 200%

192
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:38,020
of its original length.

193
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:40,420
It's in this aspect of toughness

194
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:43,460
that spider silk is really, really superior

195
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:46,420
to almost all biological materials

196
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:51,460
and really rivals or surpasses nearly all man-made materials.

197
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:55,000
There are over 39,000 species of spider,

198
00:11:55,001 --> 00:11:58,600
and each uses up to seven different types of silk,

199
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:01,000
depending on the job requirement.

200
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:05,440
A web alone is built from five different types of silk.

201
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:07,740
But one of the strongest types of spider silk

202
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:11,680
is the dragline silk, or spider's lifeline.

203
00:12:11,780 --> 00:12:14,680
The most common way to study it is to conduct a procedure

204
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:17,520
called forcible silking.

205
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:20,700
The spider is actually sedated, then secured with tape

206
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:23,500
under the microscope.

207
00:12:23,501 --> 00:12:28,000
We're manually drawing silk from a spider.

208
00:12:28,001 --> 00:12:30,000
First, you anesthetize the spider

209
00:12:30,001 --> 00:12:33,000
so that you can manipulate them without hurting yourself

210
00:12:33,001 --> 00:12:35,040
or, more importantly, hurting the spider.

211
00:12:35,041 --> 00:12:38,340
You have them breathe in carbon dioxide for a few minutes,

212
00:12:38,341 --> 00:12:40,260
and then that knocks them out.

213
00:12:40,261 --> 00:12:45,660
And then we will tape them down with their spinnerets facing up.

214
00:12:45,661 --> 00:12:48,700
We can, you know, pull on specific silk fibers,

215
00:12:48,701 --> 00:12:52,660
and we can collect them on a little spool.

216
00:12:52,661 --> 00:12:55,660
Working with spiders one at a time in the lab requires

217
00:12:55,661 --> 00:13:00,300
patience and a steady hand. But researchers are making

218
00:13:00,301 --> 00:13:05,700
progress in unraveling spider silk's tangled genetic web.

219
00:13:05,701 --> 00:13:10,220
In 1990, the first spider silk protein gene sequence was

220
00:13:10,221 --> 00:13:14,220
discovered by Ming-Chu and Randy Lewis at the University of

221
00:13:14,221 --> 00:13:17,220
Wyoming. Yet there are hundreds, perhaps

222
00:13:17,221 --> 00:13:21,660
thousands, still to discover. At this point, we know a lot of

223
00:13:21,661 --> 00:13:23,620
We know a lot about, or quite a bit about,

224
00:13:23,621 --> 00:13:26,940
the genetics underlying the spider silk proteins,

225
00:13:26,941 --> 00:13:29,300
but how we can go from those proteins

226
00:13:29,301 --> 00:13:31,600
to a spool of spider silk.

227
00:13:31,601 --> 00:13:33,940
We're working on that.

228
00:13:33,941 --> 00:13:38,200
Duplicating spider silk may be out of our reach today.

229
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:40,880
No true rope of it exists yet, but there

230
00:13:40,881 --> 00:13:44,560
is a man-made fiber that makes rope of uncommon strength.

231
00:13:44,561 --> 00:13:47,180
It's called spectra.

232
00:13:47,181 --> 00:13:49,080
When Honeywell spectra fiber was developed,

233
00:13:49,081 --> 00:13:51,780
It was a tremendous leap forward in strength.

234
00:13:51,781 --> 00:13:56,020
With this spectra fiber, we could lift something that is

235
00:13:56,021 --> 00:13:59,020
around 100, 120 pounds before the fiber actually breaks.

236
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:06,040
Spectra fiber is actually made from plastic.

237
00:14:06,041 --> 00:14:11,000
Yet it's a plastic with incredible tensile strength.

238
00:14:11,001 --> 00:14:15,200
It's a very high molecular weight polyethylene.

239
00:14:15,201 --> 00:14:19,080
Polyethylene is a long chain molecule called a polymer.

240
00:14:19,081 --> 00:14:21,580
derived from petroleum byproducts.

241
00:14:21,581 --> 00:14:23,360
Its crystalline lightweight plastics

242
00:14:23,361 --> 00:14:25,720
are ideal for rope making.

243
00:14:25,721 --> 00:14:29,260
It's manufactured using a gel spinning process.

244
00:14:29,261 --> 00:14:33,820
When we spin it, we're able to orient very long chains

245
00:14:33,821 --> 00:14:37,600
of polyethylene and really rely on the backbone

246
00:14:37,601 --> 00:14:41,660
of the polyethylene bond, which is a very strong bond.

247
00:14:41,661 --> 00:14:44,100
And because of this, we're able to generate a fiber that has

248
00:14:44,101 --> 00:14:48,540
a very high strength to weight ratio.

249
00:14:48,541 --> 00:14:53,540
creates the filament that will be spun into spectra fiber.

250
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:56,540
Hundreds of these filaments, roughly 25 microns in diameter

251
00:14:56,640 --> 00:15:01,780
each, make up a spectra fiber, which is approximately 450

252
00:15:01,880 --> 00:15:05,020
microns in diameter.

253
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:08,020
When all those spectra fibers are woven into rope,

254
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:12,600
the result is the strongest rope in the world.

255
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:15,540
Rope made from spectra fiber is seven times stronger

256
00:15:15,541 --> 00:15:19,040
stronger than steel rope, but doesn't have spider silk's

257
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:19,840
elasticity.

258
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:26,980
Throughout history, other fibers have held the title of world's

259
00:15:27,080 --> 00:15:28,880
strongest.

260
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:33,360
In prehistoric times, we used nature's bounty, such as hanging

261
00:15:33,460 --> 00:15:37,520
vines for rope, then devised methods of twisting plant fibers

262
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:40,640
to make them even stronger.

263
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:44,140
The ancient Egyptians developed special rope making tools to

264
00:15:44,141 --> 00:15:47,640
to fashion flax, grass, papyrus, and leather

265
00:15:47,740 --> 00:15:51,980
into the strong rope that raised the pyramids.

266
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:54,420
In ancient Rome, the rope of choice

267
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:57,960
was twisted hemp, which remained the leading rope fiber

268
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:00,420
until the 1800s.

269
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:03,000
Hemp grows fast, virtually anywhere,

270
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:06,260
and its fibers are easy to fashion.

271
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:08,660
But hemp had to be tarred to protect it from rotting

272
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:11,360
on the water.

273
00:16:11,361 --> 00:16:15,500
That's where abaca, also known as manila, comes in.

274
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:18,160
Manila doesn't rot nearly as fast as hemp,

275
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:22,240
and it's one of the strongest of all plant fibers.

276
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:25,880
But for the last 50 years, man-made synthetic fibers,

277
00:16:25,980 --> 00:16:29,440
such as nylon and polyester, replaced the strongest natural

278
00:16:29,540 --> 00:16:33,720
fibers for industrial rope making.

279
00:16:33,820 --> 00:16:37,260
Today, Spectra leads the synthetic fiber pack.

280
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:40,280
And at Puget Sound Rope in Anticordes, Washington,

281
00:16:40,281 --> 00:16:44,420
The manufacturer of rope using Spectra is a 24-7 operation.

282
00:16:48,720 --> 00:16:52,420
This is a creel that holds the raw material packages of

283
00:16:52,421 --> 00:16:53,121
Spectra.

284
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:57,000
This is the very beginning spot where we start to manufacture

285
00:16:57,001 --> 00:16:57,701
the rope.

286
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:02,660
When we get the fiber, every process that happens from here

287
00:17:02,661 --> 00:17:06,140
on out will reduce the efficiency of the fiber.

288
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:09,540
So it's very important that we work on keeping everything

289
00:17:09,541 --> 00:17:14,340
balanced and even. 230 spools of spectra

290
00:17:14,341 --> 00:17:20,660
fiber are pulled together for the next important step in making strong rope, the twist.

291
00:17:20,661 --> 00:17:27,300
Each one of these strands has the exact same amount of tension on it going into the twist.

292
00:17:27,301 --> 00:17:31,780
So everything will stay aligned and equal and balanced throughout the entire process,

293
00:17:32,420 --> 00:17:36,420
from here to the end user using it in their application.

294
00:17:36,421 --> 00:17:38,420
Yet as strong as spectra is,

295
00:17:38,421 --> 00:17:43,160
It can be made even stronger, actually 40% to 50% stronger.

296
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:48,560
Puget Sound Rope has a patented process that actually reorients

297
00:17:48,561 --> 00:17:51,080
the molecular structure of the spectra fiber.

298
00:17:51,380 --> 00:17:56,280
We, in essence, draw the fiber out and reorient the molecular structure

299
00:17:56,281 --> 00:18:00,480
so that the fiber is subsequently stronger than we receive it.

300
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:06,240
This highly proprietary process produces what Puget Sound Rope calls

301
00:18:06,241 --> 00:18:07,500
plasma rope.

302
00:18:07,501 --> 00:18:11,500
This is the twisted spectra fiber that we just saw on the

303
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:12,500
twister.

304
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:15,500
This now is going through the plasma process.

305
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:18,480
It will gain 50% strength, making it the strongest

306
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:21,480
rope in the world.

307
00:18:21,580 --> 00:18:23,920
The next step in any rope making process

308
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:26,380
is the highly important braid.

309
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:29,480
There are many different braid constructions.

310
00:18:29,580 --> 00:18:32,360
Each affects the performance and determines the ultimate strength

311
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:33,260
of the rope.

312
00:18:33,261 --> 00:18:40,940
For instance, one of Puget Sound's workhorse ropes has a patent on its braid construction.

313
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:45,702
This machine is a large 12-strand braider.

314
00:18:45,703 --> 00:18:49,800
There are 12 individual bobbins on the machine.

315
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:55,589
It produces a much smoother structure than conventional plane braids.

316
00:18:55,590 --> 00:18:56,820
It maximizes the

317
00:18:56,821 --> 00:18:58,960
strength and the performance properties of the structure.

318
00:18:58,961 --> 00:19:02,960
Once the strongest rope in the world is constructed,

319
00:19:02,961 --> 00:19:04,960
it needs to be mechanically tested.

320
00:19:04,961 --> 00:19:07,960
Here we're getting ready to do a destruction test

321
00:19:07,961 --> 00:19:10,960
on six-inch circumference plasma rope.

322
00:19:10,961 --> 00:19:15,960
And here we've got the fixed end of the test sample.

323
00:19:15,961 --> 00:19:17,960
We've spliced the specimen.

324
00:19:17,961 --> 00:19:20,960
One eye is here, and the other eye is attached down there.

325
00:19:20,961 --> 00:19:23,960
Since the rope is going to be used in all likelihood

326
00:19:23,961 --> 00:19:26,960
with a splice in it, some form of eye in it,

327
00:19:26,961 --> 00:19:28,960
in it so that you can connect it to something,

328
00:19:29,060 --> 00:19:31,200
hang on to something, pull something with it.

329
00:19:31,300 --> 00:19:32,960
That's how we test the rope.

330
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:40,700
And the splice has to be as perfect as the rope itself.

331
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:43,720
This precise art form is critical to the life and stability

332
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:44,620
of the rope.

333
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:50,420
Any distortion to the rope will compromise its integrity.

334
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:53,620
So a gradual taper of the splice is the strongest way to go.

335
00:19:56,960 --> 00:20:01,960
It takes 20 minutes for the machine to build up enough force

336
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:04,560
to even challenge the integrity of the rope.

337
00:20:06,340 --> 00:20:08,540
Jason, the test machine operator,

338
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:10,900
stays behind a steel wall to protect himself

339
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:12,780
